1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,500
It is midnight in a quiet city by the sea.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,500
Above you, the Moon is 384,400 kilometers away.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:17,500
It is racing sideways at one kilometer per second.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,500
That speed is the only thing keeping it in the sky.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,500
It is always falling toward us… and always missing.

6
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:32,500
Now imagine that, tonight, it simply stops.

7
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:37,500
No sideways motion. Nothing left to miss with.

8
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:42,000
There is only one way left for it to go.

9
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,800
Down.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,500
This is the Earth and the Moon, drawn to scale.

11
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,000
You could line up thirty Earths in the gap between them.

12
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Normally, the Moon travels around this circle once a month.

13
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:20,000
But if its orbital speed suddenly dropped to zero…

14
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:25,000
…it would fall straight toward us, along this line.

15
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,000
By the end of the first hour, it is moving about as fast as a cyclist.

16
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:37,500
But gravity never lets go. Every second, it falls a little faster.

17
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,500
Physics gives us an exact countdown.

18
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:52,000
From a standstill, the Moon would reach Earth in 4 days, 19 hours and 36 minutes.

19
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:57,000
But the fall is wildly uneven.

20
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:03,000
It takes almost four days just to cover the first half of the distance.

21
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:09,000
The last 100,000 kilometers take less than seven hours.

22
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:14,500
And the final 20,000? About twenty-six minutes.

23
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,300
So let’s live through it. Hour by hour.

24
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Monday morning. The city wakes up like any other day.

25
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:35,500
The Moon is already almost 2,000 kilometers closer. Nobody can tell.

26
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:41,500
But astronomers still bounce lasers off mirrors left on the Moon by Apollo astronauts.

27
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,500
Within hours, they see the impossible.

28
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:52,000
The Moon has stopped circling us. And it is coming closer.

29
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:57,000
By evening, it is the only story on Earth.

30
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:04,500
Tuesday night. The Moon rises over the sea, a little too big.

31
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:10,500
It looks about 10% wider than a normal full moon, and a fifth brighter.

32
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:15,500
Crowds gather on the beach just to look at it.

33
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:22,000
It is beautiful. And it is now falling at almost 500 meters every second.

34
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:28,000
It weighs 73 quintillion tonnes.

35
00:03:28,500 --> 00:03:34,000
No rocket, no bomb, nothing humanity has ever built could even nudge it.

36
00:03:40,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Thursday. The Moon is now 240,000 kilometers away.

37
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:51,000
Tides exist because the Moon pulls harder on the near side of Earth than on the far side.

38
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:56,500
That difference grows with the cube of the distance.

39
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Bring the Moon twice as close, and tides become eight times stronger.

40
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:07,500
Today, the tidal pull is already four times normal.

41
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:13,500
At high tide, the sea climbs past the beach, over the promenade, into the streets.

42
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:20,500
Six hours later, it drains away again — far beyond the harbor.

43
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Friday, 4 a.m. The Moon is 160,000 kilometers away.

44
00:04:28,500 --> 00:04:33,500
Tidal force: thirteen times normal.

45
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:39,500
In narrow bays, tides that once rose a few meters now rise tens of meters.

46
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Every coastal city on Earth is flooding twice a day.

47
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:51,000
Hundreds of millions of people are moving inland, all at once.

48
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:56,500
But the ocean is not the only thing being pulled.

49
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:02,000
The solid ground beneath you is rising and falling too.

50
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:08,000
Rock flexes like water. Old faults begin to slip.

51
00:05:08,500 --> 00:05:14,000
The first great earthquakes arrive just after sunrise.

52
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:30,500
Friday, noon. The Moon is 100,000 kilometers away.

53
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:36,000
Right now it is on the other side of the planet, below your feet.

54
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:42,000
Even from there, its pull is shaking the ground under you.

55
00:05:42,500 --> 00:05:48,000
Tidal stress is fifty times normal. The crust is being kneaded like dough.

56
00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:54,000
Volcanoes that slept for centuries erupt all along the Pacific Ring of Fire.

57
00:05:54,500 --> 00:05:59,500
No building on Earth was designed for this.

58
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,500
And the Moon is still more than six hours away.

59
00:06:06,500 --> 00:06:11,500
Friday evening. Sunset. And, one last time… moonrise.

60
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:17,500
It is 40,000 kilometers away. Nearly ten times wider than it used to be.

61
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:23,500
It reflects almost ninety times more sunlight than a normal full moon.

62
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:30,000
Under it, the ocean is being lifted into a mountain of water hundreds of meters high.

63
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:36,000
As Earth turns, that mountain sweeps toward every coast in its path.

64
00:06:36,500 --> 00:06:41,500
The Moon is now falling at four kilometers per second.

65
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:47,500
The air is pulled too. Winds stronger than any hurricane tear across the land.

66
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:54,000
And then, something starts to happen to the Moon itself.

67
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:20,500
19:14. The Moon crosses 18,400 kilometers.

68
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:25,000
This is the Roche limit.

69
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:31,500
Here, Earth pulls on the Moon’s near side so much harder than on its far side…

70
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:37,000
…that the Moon’s own gravity can no longer hold it together.

71
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:42,500
Its four-billion-year-old crust begins to crack.

72
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:47,500
Molten rock glows through the fractures.

73
00:07:49,500 --> 00:07:54,000
From space, it would look like this.

74
00:07:54,500 --> 00:08:00,000
The Moon stretches, then shatters into a stream of fragments.

75
00:08:00,500 --> 00:08:06,000
Some pieces are bigger than countries. All of them are still falling.

76
00:08:06,500 --> 00:08:12,500
With no orbit left, the debris cannot form a ring like Saturn’s.

77
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:18,000
It has nowhere to go but down.

78
00:08:18,500 --> 00:08:23,500
Impact: about twenty minutes from now.

79
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:35,000
The smallest fragments reach the atmosphere first.

80
00:08:35,500 --> 00:08:41,000
They hit the air at almost ten kilometers per second, and the sky catches fire.

81
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:46,500
The atmosphere itself heats until it glows.

82
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:52,500
Above you, the largest pieces now fill half the sky.

83
00:08:53,500 --> 00:08:58,500
19:36. Friday.

84
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:18,500
The impact releases about 3.6 × 10³⁰ joules of energy.

85
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:24,500
Nearly ten million times the asteroid that ended the dinosaurs.

86
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:30,000
The planet survives. Its surface does not.

87
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:36,000
Oceans boil away. The crust melts into a global sea of magma.

88
00:09:36,500 --> 00:09:41,500
For thousands of years, Earth glows like a dim star.

89
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:46,500
Life, as we know it, is gone.

90
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:53,000
Strangely, this would not be the first time.

91
00:09:53,500 --> 00:09:59,500
4.5 billion years ago, a planet the size of Mars slammed into the young Earth.

92
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:05,500
The debris from that collision came together… and became our Moon.

93
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:11,000
Born in fire. And in this story, returned in fire.

94
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:20,000
But you can relax.

95
00:10:20,500 --> 00:10:26,000
In reality, nothing can stop the Moon in its orbit.

96
00:10:26,500 --> 00:10:32,000
In fact, it is slowly drifting away from us — 3.8 centimeters every year.

97
00:10:32,500 --> 00:10:37,000
About as fast as your fingernails grow.

98
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:45,000
What if… the Moon fell to Earth?

99
00:10:47,000 --> 00:11:04,000
Thanks for watching. Which “what if” should we simulate next?

